let's talk about nato  it's the military and political alliance  set up by the west after world war ii in  response to the soviet union's growing  power we will now proceed to the signing  of the north atlantic treaty  more than 70 years later with russia's  invasion of ukraine people are now  talking about a new cold war  and nato's purpose seems to have come  full circle we face the most serious  security crisis in a generation and  therefore nato needs to respond right  now nato's focus is on its eastern front  it's sending more soldiers and equipment  to countries like lithuania where we are  we're here to see exactly what nato is  doing  it's a bit of a bumpy ride but they're  taking us to a place where some  reinforcements have just arrived  we'll find out why lithuanians feel  vulnerable when it comes to russia  and why nato's strategy now seems to be  catching up with how people here see  things  right let's go through some nato basics  to start us off nato stands for north  atlantic treaty organization and at its  core is this idea of collective defense  basically an attack against one member  is seen as an attack against all of them  if you've heard of article 5 that's  essentially what it says  now nato started off in 1949 with just  12 members today it's got 30 and the us  has always been the biggest military  power but there's also the uk most of  europe and turkey lithuania joined in  2004 along with six other countries  either all of them had been part of the  soviet union at one point or within its  sphere of influence the door to nato  will remain open  until the whole of europe  is united in freedom  and in peace  [Applause]  i think there was a lot of euphoria  after  2004 at least here  in the baltic states it seemed like the  end of the cold war  brought a lasting peace to european  continent of course the relationship  with russia was becoming increasingly  complicated back then vladimir putin had  only been president for a few years but  his worldview and ambitions to restore  russia as a global power were becoming  clearer this speech was a pretty big  clue  so putin was watching all these  countries that had all been part of the  soviet space signing up to nato and he  saw it as a major threat he accused nato  of breaking a promise to never expand  eastward but nato says there was no  promise it goes back to 1990 and a  conversation between the u.s secretary  of state and the leader of the soviet  union over the status of a unified  germany historians are still debating  what was or wasn't said and what it all  meant  there have been other critics of nato's  eastern expansion too and not only on  the russian side george kennan was a top  u.s diplomat who shaped u.s policy  against the soviet union during the cold  war in 1998 he told the new york times i  think the russians will gradually react  quite adversely and it will affect their  policies i think it is a tragic mistake  there was no reason for this whatsoever  no one was threatening anybody else now  nato didn't have much of a military  presence in the east until after 2014.  what america is officially calling a  russian invasion of ukraine russian  troops spreading out throughout the  strategic crimean peninsula after  russia's invasion and annexation of  crimea nato decided to send combat  forces to the east for the first time  it set up four battle groups each with  around a thousand soldiers in estonia  latvia lithuania and poland but now  after russia's most recent invasion of  ukraine nato's beefing up those existing  battle groups  and adding four new ones  nato is refocusing back to territorial  defense back to increasing military  capacities personnel  in in those  eastern flank  nato states  so this is essentially set up for us the  media but it's to show us the kind of  hardware that's just arrived  there are radar systems and stinger air  defense missiles the kind being used by  the ukrainian army it's basically stuff  that helps shoot other stuff out of the  sky that's how one officer put it  more soldiers have arrived too we are  now up to 1 600 1700 soldiers that means  we are reinforced by nearly 500 soldiers  right now  they're part of the 40 000 troops that  nato says are now under its direct  command stationed along its eastern  flank  but the baltic states are saying even  those kinds of numbers aren't enough  anymore  we're going to get back to nato strategy  in a bit but first let's understand why  lithuania feels vulnerable to begin with  there's the geography on one side  lithuania's got kaliningrad a russian  territory that is highly militarized  and on the other is a long border with  russia's close ally belarus which has  been a base for russian forces attacking  ukraine  and then there's the history lithuania  regained its independence in the early  90s when the soviet union collapsed  before that it had been a part of the  ussr for nearly 50 years  there aren't really many signs of that  era left but we're going to a place  where much of it's been preserved in a  theme park  [Music]  [Laughter]  [Music]  regime was brutal  massive depressions massive deportations  about 300 000 people were sent to gulat  to siberia do you remember seeing these  statues yes i do yes they do they were a  part of my childhood  my grandmother lived in vilnius and  lennon was very close to her in the red  square but i do remember the moment when  he was removed i was standing there with  my father and my dad said that's a huge  moment you know that's a huge moment  because the collapse of the soviet union  um in the 1991 that was such a  major geopolitical event which started a  new era  that new era put lithuania on a new path  one that was very different to russia's  under president putin  the  accession into the eu and nato  was very much seen as a return to  so-called the west broadly speaking  countries united by the same  principles of democratic governance and  market economies  [Music]  now if you look at recent polls  lithuanians overwhelmingly support being  part of the european union and being a  member of nato those are some pretty  strong signs of just how far the  country's moved on from its fairly  recent soviet past  [Music]  you just have to look around vilnius to  see how strongly the war in ukraine  resonates with lithuanians  there are flags everywhere on balconies  in restaurants even the buses have  messages of support  and some of that comes from a sense of  shared history and values our values are  very similar i believe that the  ukrainian people wanted freedom  they wanted fair elections they wanted  democratic institutions they wanted  transparent institutions and  because we share these values and we  know  how difficult it is sometimes to get  those values we we feel like brothers  and sisters it's like written under our  skin  what we are talking about because we are  really near to russia  we can feel it so there's quite a strong  feeling here that they understand the  issues around russia and security better  than most and leaders in this region  have been talking about what they see as  a russian threat for a long time now  well before the war in ukraine  politics  is  and nato is now rethinking its strategy  in a big way right now its battle groups  in the east serve as what's called a  tripwire defense basically enough  soldiers to slow down an invading army  but nowhere near enough to defeat it  so they're talking about a new plan  nato's secretary general is calling it a  reset moving away from tripwire  deterrence to something more about  denial or defense what he means is a  much bigger force one that would be  capable of taking on an invading army  and on top of that sweden and finland  are now thinking seriously about joining  nato too  they've both been neutral for decades  and finland joining would double the  length of nato's border with russia  it's another example of how the war in  ukraine has shifted things and not in a  way that president putin wants  we're just outside the russian embassy  in vilnius and just look at the messages  people are sending the kremlin this  reads putin the hague is waiting that  says glory to ukraine they've even  renamed this street ukrainian hero  street now whatever plan putin had in  ukraine  this overwhelming pro-ukrainian  anti-russian sentiment probably wasn't  part of it and the same goes for nato  putin has given this western military  alliance new purpose  and an even stronger foothold in what he  considers to be his backyard  [Music]  you

